meta {
  name: Shortlist a blocked candidate
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/requisitions/{{jobId}}/shortlist
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "employee_id": "{{blockedEmployeeId}}"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 409
}

tests {
  test("409 Conflict — not 400, not 422", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(409);
  });

  test("the reason names the skills that did it", function() {
    expect(res.getBody().detail).to.equal("Vikram Iyer is missing mandatory Python, SQL");
  });
}

docs {
  **The request this collection exists to show.** The payload is well-formed, the
  requisition exists, the employee exists — and the API still refuses, because Vikram is
  below the bar on two mandatory skills.

  `409` rather than `400` is a deliberate choice, and the source says why:

  > *the request is well-formed, it conflicts with the state of the requisition. That
  > distinction is what lets a client tell "you sent me nonsense" apart from "this person
  > genuinely cannot do the job".*

  Compare with the GraphQL version of this same call in
  [`fast-api-graphql/bruno`](../../fast-api-graphql/bruno), which answers **200** with a
  typed `CandidateBlocked` object. Same refusal, carried by the schema instead of the
  status line — and there, unlike here, the blocking skills arrive as a list a client can
  branch on rather than a sentence it has to parse.
}
